--TEST--
`purge` answers how many rows it removed, so a caller's drain loop terminates on that count rather than on a flag — `rule:concurrency/queue-deletion-is-explicit-and-bounded`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The count is what makes this member usable against the table it was written
// for: the first purge a deployment runs is against one that has been growing
// since it was deployed, and `while (purge(…) > 0) {}` is the shape that
// drains it one bounded statement at a time.
uint $swept = Core\Queue::purge("email");
uint $again = Core\Queue::purge("email", {limit: 500});

// And it is that count and nothing narrower. A member answering merely whether
// it had done anything would leave the correct call and the catastrophic one
// looking identical at the call site, which is the same reading `limit` being
// finite with nothing written comes from.
bool $did = Core\Queue::purge("email");
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `bool`, found `uint`
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
